Third-party components
======================

paho-mqtt
---------

  Vendored version   2.1.0
  Upstream project   https://github.com/eclipse-paho/paho.mqtt.python
  Upstream package   https://pypi.org/project/paho-mqtt/2.1.0/
  Source archive     paho_mqtt-2.1.0.tar.gz
  Archive SHA-256    12d6e7511d4137555a3f6ea167ae846af2c7357b10bc6fa4f7c3968fc1723834
  License            EDL-1.0 (BSD 3-Clause)

  paho-mqtt is dual-licensed under the Eclipse Public License 2.0 and the
  Eclipse Distribution License 1.0. This project takes it under the
  **Eclipse Distribution License 1.0**, which is a BSD 3-Clause licence and is
  compatible with the GPL.

  The package is vendored verbatim from the source archive above, with no
  modifications to its code. It lives in the tree at:

    src/MQTTBridge/_vendor/paho/              the package, as shipped upstream
    src/MQTTBridge/_vendor/paho/LICENSE.txt   upstream's licence pointer
    src/MQTTBridge/_vendor/paho/edl-v10       Eclipse Distribution License 1.0
    src/MQTTBridge/_vendor/paho/epl-v20       Eclipse Public License 2.0

  Installed on the receiver at:

    /usr/lib/enigma2/python/Plugins/Extensions/MQTTBridge/_vendor/paho/

  It sits under `_vendor/` rather than directly in the plugin directory because
  that directory is what goes on `sys.path` to make `import paho` work, and
  `sys.path[0]` is searched before the standard library for the whole enigma2
  process. Only vendored third-party packages belong there.

  It is vendored rather than declared as a dependency because Enigma2 images
  ship no pip and carry no paho package in their feeds.

  Bumping the vendored version means: re-download the archive, verify its
  SHA-256 against the PyPI JSON API, replace the package verbatim, update the
  version and hash above, and add a CHANGELOG entry.

  Nobody has to remember to look. The `vendored-paho` job in the CI workflow
  runs weekly, and on demand from the Actions tab, and compares the version
  recorded here and in src/MQTTBridge/_vendor/paho against the latest 2.x on PyPI. It is
  a soft job - `continue-on-error`, so it never blocks a merge or a release -
  and its only output is a warning on the run when this file has fallen behind.
  A vendored dependency has no package manager to nag you; this is the nag.
